This place isn't just big for an independent grocery store, it's big for any grocery store.

234
00:16:49,101 --> 00:16:53,400
It's honestly one of the largest grocery stores in existence.

235
00:16:53,401 --> 00:16:56,900
is so large that it has its own interstate exit,

236
00:16:56,901 --> 00:16:58,900
and many of the employees use segways

237
00:16:58,901 --> 00:17:01,400
to travel from one department to another.

238
00:17:01,401 --> 00:17:03,400
The store's even got a monorail

239
00:17:03,401 --> 00:17:06,400
that can transport customers from the far reaches

240
00:17:06,401 --> 00:17:09,400
of the parking lot to the store's front door.

241
00:17:09,401 --> 00:17:10,400
That's good, isn't it? Price magic.

242
00:17:10,401 --> 00:17:12,400
Now, look, see, we got Price Magic signs.

243
00:17:12,401 --> 00:17:14,400
Now, what do you think?

244
00:17:14,401 --> 00:17:16,400
That's Jungle Jim Bonominio,

245
00:17:16,401 --> 00:17:19,400
the store's owner and namesake.

246
00:17:19,401 --> 00:17:21,400
We have a different customer that comes in here.

247
00:17:21,401 --> 00:17:22,840
They're not in a rush.

248
00:17:22,841 --> 00:17:25,800
They know when they come to Jungle Gyms, it's a big place.

249
00:17:25,801 --> 00:17:28,600
They're gonna be here in a couple hours.

250
00:17:28,601 --> 00:17:32,640
Jungle Gyms carries every item a food lover could imagine.

251
00:17:32,641 --> 00:17:37,540
From the 1,400 varieties of hot sauce to the 12,000 vintages

252
00:17:37,541 --> 00:17:44,360
of wine, this megastore moves 23 million food products each year.

253
00:17:44,361 --> 00:17:47,460
But it's the staggering volume of in-house production that sets

254
00:17:47,461 --> 00:17:50,300
that sets Jungle Jim's apart from other supermarkets.

255
00:17:52,160 --> 00:17:55,500
The bakery at Jungle Jim's bakes 1,100 loaves a day,

256
00:17:56,180 --> 00:17:58,860
nearly five times the amount of your average grocery store.

257
00:18:01,160 --> 00:18:03,360
The meat department is even more astounding.

258
00:18:05,760 --> 00:18:08,360
Jungle Jim's cuts and ages more meat in one day

259
00:18:08,361 --> 00:18:10,880
than most stores do in an entire year.

260
00:18:12,580 --> 00:18:15,900
It's not just traditional fare the butchers at Jungle Jim's serve up.

261
00:18:15,901 --> 00:18:17,900
They cater to more exotic palates as well.

262
00:18:17,901 --> 00:18:19,900
We have calf head out there.

263
00:18:19,901 --> 00:18:22,900
We have hog heads, lamb heads.

264
00:18:22,901 --> 00:18:24,900
Gosh, there's a little bit of everything.

265
00:18:24,901 --> 00:18:27,900
And even though Jungle Jim's is far from the coast,

266
00:18:27,901 --> 00:18:29,900
the store prides itself on having one of the largest

267
00:18:29,901 --> 00:18:32,900
fresh seafood departments of any grocery store in America,

268
00:18:32,901 --> 00:18:35,900
complete with its own fish farm.

269
00:18:35,901 --> 00:18:37,900
We actually brought in live fish at the store

270
00:18:37,901 --> 00:18:40,900
in the seafood department.

271
00:18:40,901 --> 00:18:43,900
This is Jimmy Bonamino, Jungle Jim's son.

272
00:18:43,901 --> 00:18:48,900
He's in charge of making sure every fish the store sells is as fresh as possible.

273
00:18:48,901 --> 00:18:52,900
We're able to hold them in these tanks and keep them at optimum conditions and then bring

274
00:18:52,901 --> 00:18:55,900
them out to the customers as needed.

275
00:18:55,901 --> 00:19:02,900
The fish are housed in five separate 4,000 gallon tanks, each set to a specific temperature.

276
00:19:02,901 --> 00:19:07,900
This means that each fish is in peak condition at the time it's harvested.

277
00:19:07,901 --> 00:19:12,900
Tilapia, for instance, requires about 70 degrees of water and all the other varieties

278
00:19:12,901 --> 00:19:16,840
the tail of the catfish require to be about 58 degrees.

279
00:19:17,440 --> 00:19:20,020
So they use as little body mass as possible.

280
00:19:20,440 --> 00:19:22,200
So they're the biggest and the freshest fish

281
00:19:22,201 --> 00:19:23,460
when customers come into the aisle.

282
00:19:24,120 --> 00:19:28,420
In an average week, Jungle Jim's will net, cut, wrap,

283
00:19:28,740 --> 00:19:32,460
and ring up nearly 1,500 pounds of fresh seafood.

284
00:19:33,400 --> 00:19:36,160
This guy looks kind of feisty, but I don't blame him.

285
00:19:37,360 --> 00:19:39,940
Jungle Jim's seafood department may be massive,

286
00:19:39,941 --> 00:19:46,400
But when this gargantuan grocery store first opened its doors in the early 1970s, it didn't

287
00:19:46,401 --> 00:19:50,340
even have a seafood department, or even doors for that matter.

288
00:19:50,341 --> 00:19:54,220
I had stuff in the back of a van and I would sit on the side of the road and I would try

289
00:19:54,221 --> 00:19:55,820
to sell everything in my van.

290
00:19:55,821 --> 00:20:01,540
And if I sold it all, the next day I'd fill the van up again.

291
00:20:01,541 --> 00:20:06,760
Forty years later, Jungle Jim's is so large that dozens of customers get lost inside the

292
00:20:06,761 --> 00:20:08,800
store each day.

293
00:20:08,801 --> 00:20:11,960
In an effort to keep shoppers pointed in the right direction,

294
00:20:11,961 --> 00:20:14,660
Jungle Jim's has added massive landmarks,

295
00:20:14,661 --> 00:20:16,560
like this life-sized fishing boat.

296
00:20:16,561 --> 00:20:19,140
I mean, I had a guy right next to the seafood department

297
00:20:19,141 --> 00:20:20,860
say, where's the seafood department?

298
00:20:20,861 --> 00:20:24,300
And there's a 55-foot boat, OK?

299
00:20:24,301 --> 00:20:27,800
A full-size fire engine marks the way to the hot sauce.

300
00:20:27,801 --> 00:20:29,800
A towering giraffe overlooks the entrance

301
00:20:29,801 --> 00:20:31,680
to the garden center.

302
00:20:31,681 --> 00:20:33,920
And an animatronic Robin Hood points the way

303
00:20:33,921 --> 00:20:36,920
to Jungle Jim's eclectic English food section.

304
00:20:36,921 --> 00:20:40,020
Lords and ladies, noble foodies all.

305
00:20:40,620 --> 00:20:43,180
In fact, Jungle Jim's is so big,

306
00:20:43,660 --> 00:20:46,200
they have an entire department devoted to the construction

307
00:20:46,201 --> 00:20:48,700
of these quirky in-store guideposts.

308
00:20:51,220 --> 00:20:54,920
Welcome to the 17,000-square-foot machine shop.

309
00:20:55,920 --> 00:20:58,120
This is where we make everything.

310
00:20:58,920 --> 00:21:02,020
Here, a team of master carpenters, electricians,

311
00:21:02,021 --> 00:21:05,080
and other craftsmen create and refurbish the landmarks

312
00:21:05,081 --> 00:21:11,640
that help Jungle Jim's customers navigate this one-of-a-kind megastore's seemingly endless aisles of goods.

313
00:21:11,820 --> 00:21:13,000
It's time to get to work.

314
00:21:18,440 --> 00:21:23,160
Jungle Jim's isn't the only retail giant that's used the wow factor to become a landmark.

315
00:21:23,960 --> 00:21:29,200
Up next, a groundbreaking megastore that's cornered the market on the great outdoors

316
00:21:29,201 --> 00:21:35,300
and in the process, become the largest tourist attraction in the state of Missouri.

317
00:21:37,320 --> 00:21:41,900
More than 800,000 people visit the Washington Monument each year.

318
00:21:42,520 --> 00:21:47,120
The number of visitors to Jungle Gyms is nearly five times that.

319
00:21:48,400 --> 00:21:53,060
Megastores will return on Modern Marvels, here on History.

320
00:21:54,740 --> 00:21:58,560
We now return to Megastores on Modern Marvels.

321
00:21:59,200 --> 00:22:08,020
Whether it's aisle after aisle of groceries or hardware stacked to the rafters, most megastores

322
00:22:08,021 --> 00:22:10,300
have one objective.

323
00:22:10,301 --> 00:22:18,120
Build the biggest big box possible, fill it to the rim, and start reeling in the customers.

324
00:22:18,121 --> 00:22:21,980
But one megastore in Missouri has taken a totally different approach.

325
00:22:21,981 --> 00:22:27,600
They've packed their store with outdoor attractions and in the process become the biggest tourist

326
00:22:27,601 --> 00:22:31,564
destination in the state. Believe it or not, we're not at SeaWorld.

327
00:22:31,565 --> 00:22:33,220
We are actually at Bass Pro,

328
00:22:33,400 --> 00:22:42,740
a retail store. Welcome to the original Bass Pro shop in Springfield, Missouri.

329
00:22:43,240 --> 00:22:49,240
At a whopping 330,000 square feet, it's one of the largest outdoor stores in the world.

330
00:22:52,140 --> 00:22:56,500
And with more than 4 million visitors each year, it's also the busiest.

331
00:22:56,501 --> 00:23:03,420
It's a natural history museum, it's a conservation education center, it's an aquarium.

332
00:23:03,421 --> 00:23:04,420
It's just amazing.

333
00:23:04,421 --> 00:23:09,960
There's so much to see that it's almost like an amusement park.

334
00:23:09,961 --> 00:23:12,840
Four million people come through the doors of this store every year.

335
00:23:12,841 --> 00:23:18,020
Their chins fall down to their chest and their eyes get big and they go, wow.

336
00:23:18,021 --> 00:23:23,340
With good reason, Bass Pro has moved heaven and earth to bring outdoor sports indoors.

337
00:23:23,341 --> 00:23:33,380
For gun enthusiasts, they buried a 100-yard-long, solid concrete rifle range six feet under

338
00:23:33,381 --> 00:23:34,380
the parking lot.

339
00:23:34,381 --> 00:23:38,520
The rifle tube is a 100-yard rifle tube.

340
00:23:38,521 --> 00:23:43,260
We have target ranges from 7, 15, 25, 50, and 100 yards.

341
00:23:43,261 --> 00:23:48,840
It's one of the few long-range rifle tubes available to the public in Missouri.

342
00:23:48,841 --> 00:23:53,100
As a matter of fact, I think it's the only long-range one.

343
00:23:53,101 --> 00:24:03,340
But it's not just outdoor sports Bass Pro has brought indoors, it's the outdoors itself,

344
00:24:03,341 --> 00:24:05,040
or at least an exact replica.

345
00:24:05,041 --> 00:24:11,020
If you'll look in our boat showroom and fishing department, it very much looks like the swamps

346
00:24:11,021 --> 00:24:12,300
of the south.

347
00:24:12,301 --> 00:24:17,580
As you transcend through the store, you get into more of the Midwest, Ozarks feel.

348
00:24:17,581 --> 00:24:21,780
As you get down on this end of the building, you get into the mountain west.

349
00:24:21,781 --> 00:24:24,380
We have grizzly bears from Alaska.

350
00:24:24,381 --> 00:24:26,220
We have bighorn sheep.

351
00:24:26,221 --> 00:24:29,280
There's mountain lions in this display as well.

352
00:24:29,281 --> 00:24:32,560
In an effort to keep outdoor enthusiasts coming back,

353
00:24:32,561 --> 00:24:35,520
Bass Pro recently brought in a 9,000 pound

354
00:24:35,521 --> 00:24:37,300
bronze deer statue.

355
00:24:37,301 --> 00:24:40,200
So large, it had to be lowered in through the roof.

356
00:24:41,900 --> 00:24:44,640
As if that wasn't enough to captivate customers,

357
00:24:44,641 --> 00:24:46,640
there's a project underway to recreate

358
00:24:46,641 --> 00:24:48,580
a Missouri forest in autumn,

359
00:24:48,581 --> 00:24:50,920
complete with 35-foot tall sycamore trees.

360
00:24:51,920 --> 00:24:55,200
In the wild, it takes 80 years to grow a tree this size.

361
00:24:55,760 --> 00:24:59,060
But at Bass Pro, a team of designers working around the clock

362
00:24:59,061 --> 00:25:03,780
can create one in just two weeks out of steel, foam, and epoxy.

363
00:25:04,360 --> 00:25:07,320
Once the tree's formed, we'll go ahead and put the epoxy on it,

364
00:25:07,640 --> 00:25:10,660
apply it, and you can see I'm pressing it on.

365
00:25:10,720 --> 00:25:13,860
I'm getting the relief from a bark impression on this root down here.

366
00:25:13,861 --> 00:25:19,580
Once we're down to that part, we'll come back, we'll moss the tree, put the vines in,

367
00:25:19,620 --> 00:25:22,080
we'll bring everything back into it like it would be in nature.

368
00:25:23,620 --> 00:25:29,340
It's all part of an effort to keep the customers coming in and maintain Bass Pro's status as the

369
00:25:29,341 --> 00:25:31,663
Show Me State's favorite landmark.

370
00:25:31,664 --> 00:25:35,080
Though it's hard to believe this complex operation

371
00:25:35,081 --> 00:25:38,620
started out as a simple dream of one young fisherman.

372
00:25:39,620 --> 00:25:43,120
This whole shop started from one thing, and that's passion.

373
00:25:43,121 --> 00:25:44,960
people loving to fish.

374
00:25:45,060 --> 00:25:46,600
I grew up here in the Ozarks.

375
00:25:46,700 --> 00:25:50,900
I loved to fish, and my dad had a liquor store down the road,

376
00:25:51,540 --> 00:25:55,440
and I talked my dad into letting me put in a fishing tackle

377
00:25:55,441 --> 00:25:57,100
in his store down the street.

378
00:25:58,800 --> 00:26:01,720
Johnny Morris' love of fishing ultimately led him

379
00:26:01,721 --> 00:26:05,680
to install eight aquariums, totaling 150,000 gallons.

380
00:26:06,880 --> 00:26:09,060
The aquariums are the largest attraction

381
00:26:09,061 --> 00:26:11,180
at any of Bass Pro's 56 stores,

382
00:26:11,181 --> 00:26:15,120
and they contain 109 species of aquatic life.

383
00:26:17,680 --> 00:26:19,940
You're going to see a lot of the largemouth bass.

384
00:26:20,440 --> 00:26:25,060
You'll see smallmouth bass, lake sturgeon, walleye, scar,

385
00:26:25,240 --> 00:26:28,460
any of the fish almost that you find in the local waters you'll find here.

386
00:26:29,620 --> 00:26:32,760
The aquarium is staffed with a team of trained biologists,

387
00:26:33,280 --> 00:26:34,280
like Mitch Wakefield,

388
00:26:34,420 --> 00:26:39,260
who keeps tabs on the fish and keeps customers entertained.

389
00:26:39,261 --> 00:26:41,920
Not a lot of stores you'd find a scuba driver.

390
00:26:41,921 --> 00:26:45,100
My guess would be Bath Pro Shops would be about it.

391
00:26:45,500 --> 00:26:47,860
Pretty neat experience to see a scuba driver

392
00:26:47,861 --> 00:26:49,660
in the middle of a fish tank while shopping.

393
00:26:50,620 --> 00:26:53,340
Pick any of the 600-plus animals in the store,

394
00:26:53,780 --> 00:26:55,820
and Mitch can tell you its age, origin,

395
00:26:56,160 --> 00:26:57,780
and complete medical history,

396
00:26:58,200 --> 00:27:00,520
even if there are dozens more just like it.

397
00:27:04,780 --> 00:27:08,000
That's because each animal has been individually catalogued

398
00:27:08,001 --> 00:27:10,000
through an advanced monitoring system.

399
00:27:10,001 --> 00:27:12,000
As with all big stores, inventory is a challenge,

400
00:27:12,001 --> 00:27:14,000
trying to figure out what you have and where it is.

401
00:27:14,001 --> 00:27:18,000
The way we do that is we put a passive integrative transponder,

402
00:27:18,001 --> 00:27:20,000
a PIT tag, inside of them.

403
00:27:20,001 --> 00:27:27,000
No bigger than a grain of rice, a PIT tag houses a tiny microchip and radio transmitter.

404
00:27:27,001 --> 00:27:33,000
Once activated, the transmitter relays a code specifically programmed into each individual chip,

405
00:27:33,001 --> 00:27:35,000
and therefore, each animal.

406
00:27:35,001 --> 00:27:35,701
each animal.

407
00:27:35,702 --> 00:27:39,720
We're able to scan it to come up with a number that is unique to this snake.

408
00:27:39,721 --> 00:27:43,358
It is a 13-digit alphanumeric code just for him.

409
00:27:43,359 --> 00:27:46,920
Even with the pit tags, taking care of all this

410
00:27:46,921 --> 00:27:49,046
aquatic life is a non-stop job.

411
00:27:49,047 --> 00:27:52,680
A foreign contaminant or even a few degrees change in

412
00:27:52,681 --> 00:27:56,478
temperature can jeopardize an entire ecosystem.

413
00:27:56,479 --> 00:28:00,680
So Bass Pro depends on an enormous filtration system

414
00:28:00,681 --> 00:28:04,920
to scrub clean every fluid ounce of water a fish takes in.

415
00:28:04,921 --> 00:28:09,640
The water enters the top of this filter, pushes down through the sand, and then out the bottom,

416
00:28:09,641 --> 00:28:11,832
and then delivers it to a specific location.

417
00:28:11,833 --> 00:28:14,920
The sand is catching the detritus which keeps the water clear.

418
00:28:15,880 --> 00:28:22,440
With 150,000 gallons of water coursing through the store, it takes 30 sand filters to keep it all fresh.

419
00:28:23,720 --> 00:28:28,280
Each filter is capable of recycling a full bathtub every 35 seconds.

420
00:28:28,281 --> 00:28:32,280
But no matter how advanced a filtration system is, dirt and

421
00:28:32,281 --> 00:28:35,280
grime are bound to collect on the rocks and logs in the tank.

422
00:28:35,281 --> 00:28:39,280
That's where Bass Pro's team of scuba divers comes in,

423
00:28:39,281 --> 00:28:42,280
attacking the problem with high-powered vacuums.

424
00:28:42,281 --> 00:28:47,280
When you have a tank at home, you vacuum it, you scrub it,

425
00:28:47,281 --> 00:28:49,280
you do all that. We have to get in to do that.

426
00:28:49,281 --> 00:28:52,280
Getting to watch chores like these up close is part of what

427
00:28:52,281 --> 00:28:54,280
draws visitors to Bass Pro.

428
00:28:54,281 --> 00:28:59,180
to Bass Pro. Of course, the goal isn't just to entertain.

429
00:28:59,960 --> 00:29:01,180
By the way, we do sell retail.

430
00:29:04,620 --> 00:29:11,500
Though ringing up 11,000 customers a day while keeping tabs on 600 wild animals is no easy feat,

431
00:29:12,960 --> 00:29:18,860
it's not nearly as nasty as dealing with a mountain of trash created by 100,000 people.

432
00:29:18,861 --> 00:29:25,700
Coming up, we'll see how one megastore copes with the dirtiest job in retail and finds

433
00:29:25,701 --> 00:29:31,240
a way to put its customers to work.

434
00:29:31,241 --> 00:29:37,380
Some of Missouri's most popular tourist attractions include Bass Pro Shop with 4 million visitors,

435
00:29:37,381 --> 00:29:40,268
Bush Stadium with 3.3 million, the St.

436
00:29:40,269 --> 00:29:44,220
Louis Zoo with 3 million, and the top of the Gateway

437
00:29:44,221 --> 00:29:48,160
Arch with 1 million visitors.

438
00:29:48,161 --> 00:29:50,360
will return on Modern Marvels.

439
00:29:51,800 --> 00:29:55,480
We now return to Megastores on Modern Marvels.

440
00:30:00,380 --> 00:30:04,400
Heating and cooling costs are one of the most expensive aspects

441
00:30:04,401 --> 00:30:05,800
of operating a megastore.

442
00:30:06,580 --> 00:30:10,220
For a megastore that measures more than 4 million square feet

443
00:30:10,221 --> 00:30:13,420
and is located in one of the coldest states in the country,

444
00:30:13,920 --> 00:30:15,680
the challenge can be monumental.

445
00:30:15,681 --> 00:30:21,360
Welcome to the Mall of America in Bloomington, Minnesota.

446
00:30:23,120 --> 00:30:29,660
Thanks to its 520 retail outlets, numerous restaurants, and its indoor amusement park,

447
00:30:30,140 --> 00:30:33,740
the Mall of America is the largest retail shopping center in the country.

448
00:30:34,440 --> 00:30:39,080
Seven baseball stadiums could fit inside the mall, 258 statues of liberty,

449
00:30:39,560 --> 00:30:42,420
and 32 Boeing 747s could fit in the mall.

450
00:30:43,280 --> 00:30:44,260
It's seriously huge.

451
00:30:44,261 --> 00:30:47,400
Yeah, it's big. It's big.

452
00:30:48,160 --> 00:30:51,800
Each week, the mall welcomes 700,000 visitors.

453
00:30:52,360 --> 00:30:55,560
That's equivalent to the entire population of the city of Boston.

454
00:30:56,380 --> 00:30:59,620
The Mall of America is basically a city in and of itself.

455
00:31:00,420 --> 00:31:04,800
You'd think keeping this city-sized megastore warm during the brutal Minnesota winter

456
00:31:04,801 --> 00:31:07,660
would translate into astronomical heating costs.

457
00:31:08,380 --> 00:31:11,860
But the Mall of America has found a unique solution to the problem.

458
00:31:12,220 --> 00:31:14,160
They put their customers to work.

459
00:31:14,260 --> 00:31:17,260
And their job is heating the mall.

460
00:31:17,261 --> 00:31:19,260
Our building has no heaters.

461
00:31:19,261 --> 00:31:22,260
It's never had any heaters, no central plants, no boilers.

462
00:31:22,261 --> 00:31:25,260
And yet we can stay 72 degrees year-round,

463
00:31:25,261 --> 00:31:29,260
even when it gets to 40 below up here in the wonderful state of Minnesota.

464
00:31:29,261 --> 00:31:31,260
How is that possible?

465
00:31:31,261 --> 00:31:33,260
With the body heat of every patron.

466
00:31:33,261 --> 00:31:37,260
Daily visitors supply half the heat needed to warm the mall.

467
00:31:37,261 --> 00:31:43,260
The other half comes from the building's 350,000 square feet of skylights.

468
00:31:43,261 --> 00:31:49,080
Among the 3800 glass panels that are used to heat this building, each panel is roughly

469
00:31:49,081 --> 00:31:53,660
three foot by six foot and extra thick to protect against hail storms.

470
00:31:53,661 --> 00:31:58,600
And the unique angle of all the panels captures the sunlight at the maximum level.

471
00:31:58,601 --> 00:32:03,940
The skylights are essentially a one-way door for the sun's rays.

472
00:32:03,941 --> 00:32:08,500
Solar radiation passes through the panels as short-wave infrared light.

473
00:32:08,501 --> 00:32:13,560
Once it hits a surface inside the mall, it turns to thermal energy and takes on a longer

474
00:32:13,561 --> 00:32:18,680
wavelength which can't pass back through the skylight.

475
00:32:18,681 --> 00:32:23,520
The combination of sunlight and body heat is so efficient that maintenance crews sometimes

476
00:32:23,521 --> 00:32:26,580
have to run air conditioning in the dead of winter.

477
00:32:26,581 --> 00:32:30,960
We have plenty of air conditioners, enough to air condition about 12,000 homes, but the

478
00:32:30,961 --> 00:32:35,060
heat part of it gets taken care of because the building was built to try to contain the

479
00:32:35,061 --> 00:32:36,060
heat.

480
00:32:36,061 --> 00:32:41,900
The result is a 100% controllable climate that keeps customers shopping year-round.

481
00:32:41,901 --> 00:32:44,600
The weather's generally perfect all the time.

482
00:32:45,420 --> 00:32:50,980
But keeping all that retail space properly heated isn't even the biggest challenge facing this megastore.

483
00:32:51,680 --> 00:32:57,300
Because it receives more visitors than Graceland, the Grand Canyon, and Disney World combined,

484
00:32:57,800 --> 00:33:01,400
the biggest problem facing the Mall of America is trash.

485
00:33:01,401 --> 00:33:06,400
customers at Mall of America generate more than 20 tons of waste every day.

486
00:33:06,401 --> 00:33:10,400
And whether it's tossed into trash cans, dropped on the floor,

487
00:33:10,401 --> 00:33:14,400
or crammed down one of the building's dozens of garbage chutes,

488
00:33:14,401 --> 00:33:18,400
if it's trash and it's in the mall, it ends up here,

489
00:33:18,401 --> 00:33:22,400
in the building's lowest level, well beneath the stores,

490
00:33:22,401 --> 00:33:26,400
where the trash is being handled by the mall's waste management team.

491
00:33:26,401 --> 00:33:30,400
The workers here aren't just bagging trash and sending it to a landfill.

492
00:33:30,401 --> 00:33:38,940
We averaged probably somewhere in the neighborhood of 2,000 to 3,000 tons of cardboard recycled a year, depending on the material.

493
00:33:40,140 --> 00:33:41,820
And cardboard is just the beginning.

494
00:33:42,700 --> 00:33:49,780
Every month, up to 75 tons of aluminum and other scrap metal is hand-sorted and sent to foundries for smelting.

495
00:33:51,860 --> 00:33:55,640
Up to 4,000 tons of waste is hauled away for compost.

496
00:33:56,160 --> 00:33:59,660
And yesterday's French fry oil is tomorrow's gas.

497
00:34:00,400 --> 00:34:04,860
We average about 3,000 gallons a month of vegetable oil recycled,

498
00:34:04,861 --> 00:34:08,780
which is currently going to a manufacturer that's making biodiesel,

499
00:34:09,180 --> 00:34:12,860
which is currently running in a 50% blend in my diesel pickup truck

500
00:34:12,861 --> 00:34:14,620
parked right over on the other side of the dock.

501
00:34:14,920 --> 00:34:19,100
All the extra food from the food courts and the restaurants in the mall

502
00:34:19,101 --> 00:34:22,600
goes to a livestock program that feeds 2,500 hogs.

503
00:34:22,601 --> 00:34:32,600
All in all, the mall's waste management team recycles 4,700 tons of trash every year,

504
00:34:32,601 --> 00:34:35,600
enough to fill more than 600 garbage trucks.

505
00:34:35,601 --> 00:34:41,600
It's all part of the massive effort to keep the Mall of America squeaky clean for its customers.

506
00:34:44,600 --> 00:34:51,600
Before the Mall of America was built, local sports junkies stood in this spot to cheer on their favorite teams.

507
00:34:52,600 --> 00:34:55,360
It used to be Met Stadium. That's where this land is.

508
00:34:56,140 --> 00:34:58,300
Not far from where Home Plate used to be,

509
00:34:58,520 --> 00:35:01,140
now stands the Mall of America's Amusement Park,

510
00:35:01,440 --> 00:35:04,080
the largest indoor amusement park in the country.

511
00:35:10,280 --> 00:35:14,680
The most important job at the amusement park belongs to the safety crew.

512
00:35:15,760 --> 00:35:18,400
Every morning, four hours before the mall opens,

513
00:35:18,401 --> 00:35:23,060
a team of master mechanics personally inspects every inch of every ride.

514
00:35:24,900 --> 00:35:28,320
Just one loose bolt can compromise an entire structure,

515
00:35:28,960 --> 00:35:33,640
and some rides have more than 1,500 bolts that need inspecting.

516
00:35:34,540 --> 00:35:39,480
So mechanics use painted markers to ensure every bolt is properly torqued.

517
00:35:40,140 --> 00:35:43,440
We can look at these in a very short period of time.

518
00:35:43,441 --> 00:35:47,440
We can look at all those without having to really get up on a

519
00:35:47,441 --> 00:35:51,440
ladder and making sure that they are indeed at their proper

520
00:35:51,441 --> 00:35:53,440
torque. Workers also inspect the

521
00:35:53,441 --> 00:35:57,440
undercarriage of 42 roller coaster cars to ensure brakes

522
00:35:57,441 --> 00:36:00,440
are bolted down tight and the base is securely fastened to

523
00:36:00,441 --> 00:36:05,440
the track. Car is in one piece.

524
00:36:05,441 --> 00:36:11,440
Anti-rollback, road wheels, guide wheels, upstop wheels,

525
00:36:11,441 --> 00:36:18,440
wheels all looks good so we'll run some cars around.

526
00:36:18,441 --> 00:36:23,440
The safety inspection has to be completed before the mall opens for

527
00:36:23,441 --> 00:36:36,440
business and before the amusement parks 12,000 daily visitors start pouring in.

528
00:36:36,441 --> 00:36:40,440
Coming up, we'll go behind the scenes at America's original

529
00:36:40,441 --> 00:36:44,440
megastore and see how a team of guard dogs keeps watch over 2

530
00:36:44,441 --> 00:36:48,440
million square feet of merchandise.

531
00:36:48,441 --> 00:36:52,440
If a shopper spent just 10 minutes in every store at the

532
00:36:52,441 --> 00:36:57,440
Mall of America, it would take 3 1⁄2 straight days to cover

533
00:36:57,441 --> 00:37:02,440
the entire mall. Megastores will return on Modern Marvels.

534
00:37:02,441 --> 00:37:07,440
We now return to Megastores on Modern Marvels.

535
00:37:12,440 --> 00:37:17,440
Every megastore is also a mega-building, a superstructure

536
00:37:17,441 --> 00:37:20,440
measuring hundreds of thousands of square feet,

537
00:37:20,441 --> 00:37:23,440
sprawled across giant swaths of suburban tract land.

538
00:37:26,440 --> 00:37:28,440
But not every megastore builds out.

539
00:37:28,441 --> 00:37:30,440
Some build straight up, including the

540
00:37:30,441 --> 00:37:35,440
build straight up, including the megastore that started it all more than a century ago.

541
00:37:36,720 --> 00:37:42,260
Welcome to Macy's and Herald Square. Measuring more than 2 million square feet,

542
00:37:43,020 --> 00:37:47,004
Macy's is one of the largest apartment stores in the world.

543
00:37:47,005 --> 00:37:49,840
Established in 1902, it's also the oldest.

544
00:37:50,900 --> 00:37:55,140
Before the subways, before everything, Macy's Herald Square was here.

545
00:37:55,141 --> 00:37:58,040
Macy's is really the mother of all megastores.

546
00:37:58,041 --> 00:38:02,380
And because the store itself dates back to the beginning of the 20th century, it is a

547
00:38:02,381 --> 00:38:07,260
concept that has remained technically on the cutting edge of innovation of what a megastore

548
00:38:07,261 --> 00:38:08,260
should be.

549
00:38:08,261 --> 00:38:13,600
When you think of shopping in New York City, the first thing that pops into your mind is

550
00:38:13,601 --> 00:38:16,440
Macy's and Macy's Herald Square.

551
00:38:16,441 --> 00:38:22,080
On a typical day, you will see maybe up to 75,000 people who come in through our doors.

552
00:38:22,081 --> 00:38:26,580
But during the peak holiday season, you see about 150,000 people.

553
00:38:27,760 --> 00:38:35,800
So how does a store that welcomes nearly 30 million visitors a year manage to squeeze into one of the most congested city blocks in America?

554
00:38:36,340 --> 00:38:41,800
The megastores that we interact with today are, for the most part, laid out horizontally, but not Macy's.

555
00:38:42,540 --> 00:38:51,740
Instead, Macy's is packed straight up into 25 floors of prime Manhattan real estate, roughly half of which is devoted to retail.

556
00:38:52,080 --> 00:38:57,080
How does Macy's keep all its customers moving seamlessly from floor to floor?

557
00:38:57,081 --> 00:39:02,080
Our extensive vertical systems are critical to moving all the customers through the building.

558
00:39:02,081 --> 00:39:05,080
We have 72 escalators in the building.

559
00:39:05,081 --> 00:39:15,080
Moving it up to 150 feet per minute, each of Macy's 72 escalators makes approximately 160,000 revolutions annually.

560
00:39:15,081 --> 00:39:21,320
At that rate, in just one year, this transportation system could move the entire population of

561
00:39:21,321 --> 00:39:24,580
New York City through the store three times over.

562
00:39:24,581 --> 00:39:28,620
The mechanics of an escalator are quite simple.

563
00:39:28,621 --> 00:39:33,080
Like a bike chain, you have two sprockets, one on the bottom, one at the top.

564
00:39:33,081 --> 00:39:38,840
You have interleaved stairs that run between them, and it runs constantly.

565
00:39:38,841 --> 00:39:42,040
Escalators in department stores are something we've come to take for granted.

566
00:39:42,041 --> 00:39:48,440
But at the dawn of the 20th century, the concept was revolutionary.

567
00:39:48,441 --> 00:39:53,280
The earliest escalators and the history of department stores actually dates back here

568
00:39:53,281 --> 00:39:55,640
to Macy's Herald Square.

569
00:39:55,641 --> 00:39:59,360
More people moving through the store meant more profit.

570
00:39:59,361 --> 00:40:03,840
And Macy's, like New York City, started growing at light speed.

571
00:40:03,841 --> 00:40:07,260
New York City developed into this large commercial hub.

572
00:40:07,261 --> 00:40:10,380
And it's thanks in part to commercial retailers like Macy's.

573
00:40:10,381 --> 00:40:15,540
They made high volume commercial retail space possible through the use of vertical transports

574
00:40:15,541 --> 00:40:18,220
like the escalator.

575
00:40:18,221 --> 00:40:22,260
Escalators are just part of the transportation system needed to move people and products

576
00:40:22,261 --> 00:40:26,320
through this vertical megastore.

577
00:40:26,321 --> 00:40:31,380
With the arrival of more than 400 tons of merchandise per hour, Macy's also relies on

578
00:40:31,381 --> 00:40:35,740
one of the largest systems of freight elevators in the world.

579
00:40:35,741 --> 00:40:41,400
of Macy's 22 freight elevators and lift up to 20,000 pounds of merchandise at a time.

580
00:40:41,880 --> 00:40:46,320
The four largest can accommodate an entire tractor trailer load of product,

581
00:40:46,780 --> 00:40:50,740
which allows Macy's to receive deliveries without adding to the congestion

582
00:40:50,741 --> 00:40:53,180
on one of New York City's busiest streets.

583
00:40:54,120 --> 00:41:00,200
In the middle of Midtown Manhattan, 34th Street, very, very busy intersection with Broadway and 34th Street.

584
00:41:00,201 --> 00:41:06,120
And that is the main reason why we do have to take in such a large amount of trailer.

585
00:41:07,380 --> 00:41:10,880
Every day, each elevator makes 50 trips,

586
00:41:11,200 --> 00:41:15,200
carrying stock to the main processing center, located on the 10th floor.

587
00:41:15,580 --> 00:41:17,180
Comes in on ground level.

588
00:41:19,740 --> 00:41:22,620
Goes up vertically 10 flights.

589
00:41:24,780 --> 00:41:30,040
And it's all being processed right here, over 70,000 pallets.

590
00:41:30,200 --> 00:41:34,200
roughly 25 million units a year.

591
00:41:34,201 --> 00:41:37,200
And it's here that each of those 25 million items

592
00:41:37,201 --> 00:41:41,200
are individually tagged and sorted before taking a ride back down

593
00:41:41,201 --> 00:41:44,200
to one of the many departments below.

594
00:41:44,201 --> 00:41:47,200
But transportation isn't the only problem facing Macy's.

595
00:41:47,201 --> 00:41:52,200
In the 1950s, store owners realized that having 2 million square feet

596
00:41:52,201 --> 00:41:58,200
of retail spread over 11 floors meant having to deal with more nooks and crannies

597
00:41:58,201 --> 00:42:02,420
and more places for potential robbers to hide from security guards.

598
00:42:03,200 --> 00:42:05,600
So Macy's decided to try something different.

599
00:42:11,000 --> 00:42:14,940
Meet Max, one of the secret guardians of Macy's.

600
00:42:16,000 --> 00:42:20,100
He's part of a pack of German shepherds who reside in the store's rooftop kennel.

601
00:42:22,780 --> 00:42:27,200
Max and the rest of the pack are trained by the same team that supplies the NYPD

602
00:42:27,201 --> 00:42:29,460
with their canine officers.

603
00:42:29,461 --> 00:42:31,600
The dogs have a keen sense of smell,

604
00:42:31,601 --> 00:42:34,300
which helps them sniff out unwelcome visitors,

605
00:42:34,301 --> 00:42:38,640
and Max can pinpoint a sound in 6 1-hundredths of a second,

606
00:42:38,641 --> 00:42:43,040
then take off toward it at 30 miles per hour.

607
00:42:43,041 --> 00:42:46,060
If they encounter somebody, they need to explain to myself

608
00:42:46,061 --> 00:42:48,960
or the handler, and more importantly Max,

609
00:42:48,961 --> 00:42:51,460
what they're doing there, and we generally don't have

610
00:42:51,461 --> 00:42:52,800
any arguments.

611
00:42:52,801 --> 00:42:55,360
It's tough to win an argument with a 90-pound,

612
00:42:55,361 --> 00:43:00,360
German Shepherd. From 10 p.m. to 7 a.m., the Macy's

613
00:43:00,361 --> 00:43:04,360
K9 unit will scour the store in search of suspicious activity.

614
00:43:04,361 --> 00:43:08,360
Just before sunup, they're taken back to their rooftop pad to rest

615
00:43:08,361 --> 00:43:11,360
and relax. Most customers will never see them,

616
00:43:11,361 --> 00:43:14,360
but they're there. They're always there. And they're another

617
00:43:14,361 --> 00:43:18,360
example of how the mother of all megastores has mastered the

618
00:43:18,361 --> 00:43:20,360
challenge of its vertical design.

619
00:43:25,360 --> 00:43:35,680
Whether stacked to the sky or spread across acres, it takes more than square footage to

620
00:43:35,681 --> 00:43:37,960
make a megastore.

621
00:43:37,961 --> 00:43:47,100
It takes cutting-edge technology, groundbreaking logistics, and above all, manpower.

622
00:43:47,101 --> 00:43:54,120
As the wheels of commerce keep turning, entrepreneurs, engineers, and of course customers will continue

623
00:43:54,121 --> 00:43:57,200
to push megastores to even greater extremes.

